  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi }\\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi }.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the factor $\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\u21d2\\,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi <-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi $ is always negative, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi } & \\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi } \\\\ (\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi } & \\le (\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(x)-\\pi } \\\\ 2x+3 & \\le 2-7x \\\\ 9x & \\le -1 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
